# ADR-002: Transition from Blog to NFL Games and Statistics Website
## Status: Accepted (Date: 2026-10-07)
## Context
The project is currently an Astro blog, but the intended product is an accessible NFL games and statistics website. The site should help visitors quickly see what is happening this week and then browse to detailed game, team, player, standings, and comparison information without overwhelming them with statistics up front.

The application needs current and historical NFL data. Calling an external provider from every visitor's browser would multiply requests, expose the site to provider availability and rate limits, and make the data source difficult to change. Live scores have different freshness needs from historical and season statistics. The `nfl-data-api` is the selected starting source for schedules, completed games, and statistics. ESPN's public-facing endpoints are not treated as a stable or supported API.

ADR-001 already selects Astro with the Node adapter in standalone mode and a container runtime. This decision builds on that server-rendered deployment.
## Decision
- Reorient the website from a blog into an NFL games and statistics explorer, retaining Astro SSR and the containerized Node runtime.
- Use `nfl-data-api` as the initial provider for schedules, completed games, historical data, standings, and statistics. Keep provider-specific requests and response mapping behind a server-side data-source boundary so providers can be replaced without coupling page components to their schemas.
- Fetch and validate provider data on the server, transform it into application-facing data, and persist or cache it for Astro pages to read. Start with a scheduled daily refresh for non-live data; make refresh frequency configurable as requirements and provider limits are confirmed.
- Treat live scores as a separate, best-effort data path. ESPN's public scoreboard and summary provide game state, scores, latest play/situation, and available live team/player box-score data. The `/api/live/games` server route validates and normalizes those responses, requests scoreboards for UTC yesterday/today/tomorrow, and caches scoreboard responses for 10 seconds and game summaries for 60 seconds in process memory with concurrent-request coalescing. The browser polls this same-origin route every 10 seconds during live games, every 60 seconds within 90 minutes of kickoff, and every five minutes otherwise; polling pauses while the tab is hidden. The NFL Data API documents a 100-request/minute/IP limit for its own API; it does not apply to ESPN, whose public endpoints have no published dependable quota. With three scoreboard date requests per active cache window, one process makes up to 18 scoreboard requests/minute during games plus approximately one summary request per active game/minute; each additional app process/task may multiply this traffic. Keep the browser behind the server cache and treat higher freshness as a conservative, configurable best-effort trade-off, not a provider capacity guarantee. Animate only detected score changes and key actions; represent LIVE as a persistent, non-blinking state. Show possession and available down/distance/red-zone information without fabricating missing values. Clearly label halftime and the two-minute warning when ESPN reports it or the period clock reaches 2:00 in the second/fourth quarter. Give detected touchdowns a distinct celebratory gold banner with the scoring team's accent, separate from penalty/review notices and ordinary key-play alerts. Map offense from ESPN's `situation.possession` team ID to competitor IDs; do not infer possession from `possessionText`, which may name the team whose yard line is displayed. Color the possession underline with that offense's team accent. Render a compact offense-oriented mini-field only in homepage active-game ticker rows, with end zones and 10-yard markers; integrate a small direction arrow beside the current ball spot pointing toward the opponent's end zone, and link each ticker row to its matching game-detail page. Omit the field graphic from the game-detail live-updates row. Plot the ball only when ESPN reports a valid integral 0–100 yard line, and clearly label missing position data. Display live team totals and available player statistics in visible tables, rather than collapsing stat categories by default. Distinguish red-zone rows with a restrained background tint/watermark, independently of LIVE and scoring animations. Keep `/api/live` unchanged as the container health endpoint.
- The mini-field uses ESPN's 0–100 field coordinate as a fixed left-to-right axis and positions the ball directly from that validated coordinate. Establish team scoring direction only from a positive-yardage play by the current offense; ignore penalties, incomplete passes, sacks, losses, and plays by a previous possession team. Remember each team's first-half scoring direction in the browser and keep it stable for that half, reversing it only after the end switch at halftime. Preserve a known ball spot even when direction is unknown, but do not provide an offense-relative spot label or arrow in that case.
- On the live game ticker, show a restrained in-card `FLAG` banner only when the latest play is explicitly a penalty/flag, or `UNDER REVIEW` when the scoreboard/play explicitly reports an active review. Prefer review when both signals are present. Style the flag with a compact yellow-and-dark broadcast-inspired strip and include the penalty description when available. Do not infer either banner from ordinary play text or animate it as a scoring event.
- Keep the homepage ticker's mini-fields aligned in shared grid columns regardless of score width or optional game-status details. Use fixed-height expanded cards and a stable field row so halftime, flags, or scoring alerts do not shift field spacing. Provide a minimal per-match chevron control that collapses/expands that game's secondary live details while preserving matchup and score. Allow users to reorder live games by hold-and-drag or keyboard shortcuts; retain each collapse choice and ordering across polling refreshes.
- For schedule cards, upcoming ticker entries, countdown details, and game pages, display only the kickoff clock time formatted in the visitor's browser timezone. Do not append timezone abbreviations or duplicate the provider/server timezone; show a pending label when no authoritative absolute kickoff timestamp is available.
- Current live refresh configuration supersedes the initial cadence above: active score/status polling and its process-local scoreboard cache use 10 seconds, while summaries remain cached for 60 seconds. This yields up to 18 scoreboard date requests per minute per app process; ESPN publishes no dependable quota, so watch for throttling or errors. When a detected score change is a touchdown, show a distinct gold celebratory `TOUCHDOWN` banner with the scoring team's accent and updated score; keep it visually separate from penalty/review notices.
- Design the interface for progressive detail: show a compact weekly overview first, then provide dedicated game, team, and player pages for deeper statistics. Make standings easy to browse; add comparisons where the available data supports them. Clearly label live status and data freshness.
## Consequences
- Positive: The product has a clear NFL focus and supports both quick browsing and deeper exploration.
- Positive: Server-side caching limits duplicate provider requests, reduces dependence on upstream availability during page views, and provides a practical data-ingestion and operations workflow.
- Positive: A provider boundary allows the historical/statistical source and any live-data source to evolve independently.
- Negative: Ingestion, validation, persistence, cache freshness, and error handling add operational complexity beyond a static blog.
- Negative: The community-maintained primary API and ESPN's public endpoints may change, become unavailable, or have data gaps. Live statistics may be missing or delayed and are not guaranteed.
- Negative: Browser polling creates additional server and upstream traffic during game windows; process-local caching limits, but does not eliminate, this load, and additional ECS tasks multiply provider requests. It is polling-based and may detect a change up to one interval late. ESPN does not publish a dependable quota.

If a live scoreboard or summary refresh fails, surface the unavailable/delayed state and continue retrying; do not silently present stale data as current. The public ESPN endpoints are best effort and may be changed or withdrawn without notice. Revisit endpoint terms, rate limits, and appropriateness if traffic or product requirements grow.